    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d41">

                <pb facs="0103" n="81" sameAs="frus:frus1961-63v16/pg81"/>

                <head>41. National Security Action Memorandum No. 75<anchor n="0" xml:id="d41-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1961-08-20">August 20,
                        1961</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list type="to">
                    <head>TO</head>
                    <item>The Secretary of State</item>
                </list>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>Polish Government Taxes on Relief Packages</item>
                </list>
                <p>Is there anything we can properly do to persuade the Polish Government to lift
                    their present restrictions in taxes which Polish recipients of a relief package
                    must pay. I understand that shipment of used clothing makes up the bulk of the
                        traffic.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d41-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">John F. Kennedy</hi><anchor n="2"
                            xml:id="d41-f3"/></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d41-f1" n="0" type="source" xml:id="d41-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, S/S–NSC Files: Lot 72 D 316, NSAM 75. Confidential.
                    A copy was sent to the Director, USIA.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d41-f2" n="1" xml:id="d41-f2-content">
                <p>In a memorandum to the President, October 19, Secretary Rusk reported that Polish
                    customs levies had been reduced and that discussions with approximately 150
                    recipients of these packages produced only three complaints, all related to
                    duties on new clothing imports. “The Embassy expressed its continuing belief
                    that the new tariff presents no difficulty to recipients of normal gift
                    parcels.” (Ibid.)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d41-f3" n="2" xml:id="d41-f3-content">
                <p>Printed from a copy that bears this typed signature.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
